     "body": "**Preserving provenance and handling uncertainty** (multi-source synthesis):\n\n- **Claim-source mapping**: every claim in a synthesis report carries **which source it came from** (source ID, URL, the passage). It is carried up as structure from the subagent stage\n- **Dates (temporal data)**: information has a point in time. Distinguish “as of 2024” from “now”; when old and new conflict, take the newer while stating both dates\n- **Annotating conflicts**: when sources disagree, **do not silently drop one**. Annotate “A reports X, B reports Y; the reason is unknown” and give the reader the material to judge\n- **Coverage gaps**: state what could not be investigated and which sources could not be retrieved (as in 5.3)\n- Confidence in a claim (several independent sources agree → high; only one → low)\n\n### 🎓 Points to remember\n- The problem: findings are compressed at the summarizing stage, and **the link between claim and source is lost**\n- Responses: have subagents output source URL, document name and the relevant excerpt as a mapping, and have the synthesis agent keep that mapping when combining / **show disagreements side by side** (do not pick one arbitrarily; annotate with sources) / **require dates** (publication, collection; do not mistake a difference in time for a contradiction) / separate sections for established and contested findings / **leave the decision to the coordinator** (document analysis finishes with the conflicting values included and annotated, and the coordinator decides how to reconcile) / fit the form to the content (financials as a table, news as prose, technical findings as a structured list)\n\n> “Sources have disappeared from the synthesis report” → make the source a required field of subagent output. “For conflicting information, keep one side by majority vote” → wrong (annotate and show both).",
